{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MA33ZctnHgAoEYmUvWb4Bq","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tname: LLM Evaluation Variance — Writing Patterns\n3\tdescription: Narrative strategies, figure/table conventions from LLM evaluation variance papers (8 deep-read, 2024-2026)\n4\ttype: writing\n5\tsource_papers: 8\n6\t---\n7\t\n8\t## Story Types\n9\t\n10\t- **Breaking limitations** (3/8): Brittlebench, Yuan, ThinkingMachines. \"Everyone assumed X, we show it fails.\" High.\n11\t- **Simplification** (2/8): Hua, GeneralizIT. \"Complex problem, simpler real answer.\" Medium.\n12\t- **Unifying framework** (2/8): Kunievsky, Messina. \"Scattered observations, one formalism.\" Medium.\n13\t- **Cross-domain transfer** (1/8): Mesting (psychometrics→ML). Our paper fits this type — study Mesting's structure. Low but critical.\n14\t\n15\t## Hook: Counter-Intuitive Claim Dominates (4/8, high)\n16\t\n17\t\"Greedy≠deterministic\" (Yuan), \"80/1000 unique at T=0\" (ThinkingMachines), \"sensitivity is artifact\" (Hua), \"95% CIs→79% coverage\" (Mesting). **Pattern**: open with a concrete number violating expectations. Avoid vague \"X is important.\"\n18\t\n19\t## Tension: \"Everyone's Doing It Wrong\" (4/8, high)\n20\t\n21\t(1) Cite widespread practice, (2) quantified evidence it fails, (3) identify overlooked factor. ThinkingMachines adds a twist: debunk the accepted explanation before presenting the correct one.\n22\t\n23\t## Intro: 4 Paragraphs + 3 Contributions (mode)\n24\t\n25\t(1) Context, (2) existing approaches + limitation, (3) gap/surprise, (4) contributions. 3 contributions standard: framework + empirical finding + practical tool. **Support: 5/8, high.**\n26\t\n27\t## Figure Conventions\n28\t\n29\t**Figure 1**: problem_illustration (4/8) — show problem exists before solution. Concrete examples with before/after or divergence visualization. **High.**\n30\t\n31\t**Result figures**: bar charts primary (5/8); stacked bars for variance decomposition (2/8, Mesting/Brittlebench — expected idiom for our paper); line plots for trends (3/8); heatmaps for divergence (2/8). **High.**\n32\t\n33\t**Figure count**: 4-6 total. **Flow**: problem illustration → method diagram → variance decomposition → D-study projections. **Support: 6/8, high.**\n34\t\n35\t## Table Conventions\n36\t\n37\t**Main table**: rows=models/configs, cols=benchmarks×metrics, grouped by category (commercial/open or reasoning/non-reasoning). Bold best, 4-8 baselines. **Support: 5/8, high.**\n38\t\n39\t**Ablation**: full_numbers (5/8) over checkmark_grid (2/8). Checkmark only for component-presence ablation. **High.**\n40\t\n41\t**Reporting**: std across configs/runs standard; only Mesting reports full REML components. **High.**\n42\t\n43\t**Figure vs table split**: figures for decomposition visuals/trends/qualitative; tables for exact numbers/comparisons. Appendix for per-config breakdowns and validation. **Support: 6/8, high.**\n44\t\n45\t## Our Paper Blueprint\n46\t\n47\tOpen with shocking quantified finding about unreported variance → \"everyone's CIs are wrong\" tension → G-theory as borrowed lens → 2-3 domain demonstrations → stacked-bar variance decomposition as signature visual → D-study projections as practical value.\n48\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4612c7a0-c8ae-42af-a27e-32f19ee90526","uuid":"38d3a8a7-69f6-4e13-bd10-556e3c012822","timestamp":"2026-05-20T19:21:09.477Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0519/skills/projects/nlp_variance_gtheory/distilled-writing.skill.md","content":"---\nname: LLM Evaluation Variance — Writing Patterns\ndescription: Narrative strategies, figure/table conventions from LLM evaluation variance papers (8 deep-read, 2024-2026)\ntype: writing\nsource_papers: 8\n---\n\n## Story Types\n\n- **Breaking limitations** (3/8): Brittlebench, Yuan, ThinkingMachines. \"Everyone assumed X, we show it fails.\" High.\n- **Simplification** (2/8): Hua, GeneralizIT. \"Complex problem, simpler real answer.\" Medium.\n- **Unifying framework** (2/8): Kunievsky, Messina. \"Scattered observations, one formalism.\" Medium.\n- **Cross-domain transfer** (1/8): Mesting (psychometrics→ML). Our paper fits this type — study Mesting's structure. Low but critical.\n\n## Hook: Counter-Intuitive Claim Dominates (4/8, high)\n\n\"Greedy≠deterministic\" (Yuan), \"80/1000 unique at T=0\" (ThinkingMachines), \"sensitivity is artifact\" (Hua), \"95% CIs→79% coverage\" (Mesting). **Pattern**: open with a concrete number violating expectations. Avoid vague \"X is important.\"\n\n## Tension: \"Everyone's Doing It Wrong\" (4/8, high)\n\n(1) Cite widespread practice, (2) quantified evidence it fails, (3) identify overlooked factor. ThinkingMachines adds a twist: debunk the accepted explanation before presenting the correct one.\n\n## Intro: 4 Paragraphs + 3 Contributions (mode)\n\n(1) Context, (2) existing approaches + limitation, (3) gap/surprise, (4) contributions. 3 contributions standard: framework + empirical finding + practical tool. **Support: 5/8, high.**\n\n## Figure Conventions\n\n**Figure 1**: problem_illustration (4/8) — show problem exists before solution. Concrete examples with before/after or divergence visualization. **High.**\n\n**Result figures**: bar charts primary (5/8); stacked bars for variance decomposition (2/8, Mesting/Brittlebench — expected idiom for our paper); line plots for trends (3/8); heatmaps for divergence (2/8). **High.**\n\n**Figure count**: 4-6 total. **Flow**: problem illustration → method diagram → variance decomposition → D-study projections. **Support: 6/8, high.**\n\n## Table Conventions\n\n**Main table**: rows=models/configs, cols=benchmarks×metrics, grouped by category (commercial/open or reasoning/non-reasoning). Bold best, 4-8 baselines. **Support: 5/8, high.**\n\n**Ablation**: full_numbers (5/8) over checkmark_grid (2/8). Checkmark only for component-presence ablation. **High.**\n\n**Reporting**: std across configs/runs standard; only Mesting reports full REML components. **High.**\n\n**Figure vs table split**: figures for decomposition visuals/trends/qualitative; tables for exact numbers/comparisons. Appendix for per-config breakdowns and validation. **Support: 6/8, high.**\n\n## Our Paper Blueprint\n\nOpen with shocking quantified finding about unreported variance → \"everyone's CIs are wrong\" tension → G-theory as borrowed lens → 2-3 domain demonstrations → stacked-bar variance decomposition as signature visual → D-study projections as practical value.\n","numLines":48,"startLine":1,"totalLines":48}}}
